{
 "war": "first-crusade",
 "start": "1096-08-01",
 "end": "1099-08-12",
 "note": "Crusader and Fatimid holdings are drawn as occupied areas over the base map, which lacks them before 1099; a moving circle marks the ground held by the marching crusader army. Areas and arrows are approximate reconstructions from campaign maps and the chronicles.",
 "chapters": [
  {
   "id": "peoples",
   "title": "The People's Crusade",
   "start": "1096-08-01",
   "end": "1096-10-31",
   "bbox": [
    28.7,
    40.0,
    30.4,
    41.2
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "The People's Crusade is ferried across to Civetot (August 1096)",
    "German crusaders raid past Nicaea and seize Xerigordos (September 1096)"
   ],
   "text": "Ahead of the princes' armies, tens of thousands of poorly armed pilgrims led by Peter the Hermit reached Constantinople, and in August 1096 the emperor Alexios ferried them across to a camp at Civetot. A German band raided towards Nicaea, the Seljuk capital, and seized the fort of Xerigordos, but the Turks besieged it and it surrendered on 29 September. On 21 October the main body marched out of Civetot into an ambush and was destroyed."
  },
  {
   "id": "nicaea",
   "title": "Nicaea and Dorylaeum",
   "start": "1097-04-26",
   "end": "1097-07-15",
   "bbox": [
    28.7,
    39.4,
    31.2,
    41.2
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "The princes' armies march from Constantinople to Nicaea (spring 1097)",
    "Kilij Arslan's attempt to relieve Nicaea (21 May 1097)",
    "Crusaders march from Nicaea to Dorylaeum (late June 1097)"
   ],
   "text": "In spring 1097 the princes' armies crossed into Asia and on 14 May besieged Nicaea, beating off Sultan Kilij Arslan's relief attack. On 19 June the city surrendered, not to the crusaders but to the Byzantine emperor, who began to win back western Asia Minor. Marching on, the crusaders defeated Kilij Arslan at Dorylaeum on 1 July 1097, which opened the road across Anatolia."
  },
  {
   "id": "anatolia",
   "title": "Across Anatolia",
   "start": "1097-07-02",
   "end": "1097-10-20",
   "bbox": [
    29.8,
    36.4,
    38.4,
    40.2
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "The main army marches across Anatolia to Antioch (July–October 1097)",
    "Tancred and Baldwin cross the Taurus into Cilicia (September 1097)"
   ],
   "text": "The main army marched across the dry Anatolian plateau in summer heat, passing Iconium and beating the Turks again near Heraclea. Tancred and Baldwin of Boulogne split off through the Taurus mountains and took Tarsus, Adana and Mamistra in Cilicia in September 1097. The main army swung north through Armenian lands to Marash, where Baldwin left it, and reached Antioch on 20 October 1097. The moving circle marks the ground held by the marching army, not a conquest."
  },
  {
   "id": "antioch",
   "title": "The siege of Antioch",
   "start": "1097-10-20",
   "end": "1098-07-31",
   "bbox": [
    35.6,
    35.8,
    39.4,
    37.6
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Baldwin rides east to Turbessel and Edessa (1097–1098)",
    "Kerbogha's army marches from Mosul to Antioch (May–June 1098)"
   ],
   "text": "Antioch's walls were too strong to storm, and the crusaders starved through a winter siege while beating off relief armies from Damascus and Aleppo. Baldwin, meanwhile, took Turbessel and on 10 March 1098 became ruler of Edessa, the first crusader state. On 3 June 1098 a traitor let Bohemond's men into Antioch; days later Kerbogha of Mosul besieged them in turn, but on 28 June the crusaders marched out and routed his army."
  },
  {
   "id": "maarra",
   "title": "Ma'arra and the road south",
   "start": "1098-11-27",
   "end": "1099-05-13",
   "bbox": [
    35.3,
    34.2,
    37.3,
    36.4
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Raymond's army leaves Ma'arra and marches south to Arqa (January–February 1099)"
   ],
   "text": "While the leaders quarrelled over Antioch, which Bohemond kept, Raymond of Toulouse stormed Ma'arra on 11–12 December 1098 and massacred its people; chroniclers report starving crusaders eating the dead. On 13 January 1099 Raymond burned Ma'arra and led the march south, and local emirs paid him to pass. From 14 February to 13 May the army besieged Arqa without success, then moved on towards Jerusalem."
  },
  {
   "id": "jerusalem",
   "title": "Jerusalem and Ascalon",
   "start": "1099-05-13",
   "end": "1099-08-12",
   "bbox": [
    34.2,
    31.2,
    35.8,
    33.6
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "The crusaders march down the coast and inland to Ramla (May–June 1099)",
    "Tancred rides to Bethlehem (6 June 1099)",
    "The army arrives before Jerusalem (7 June 1099)"
   ],
   "text": "The army marched down the coast past the Fatimid ports, turned inland and took Ramla, which its people had abandoned, on 3 June 1099; Tancred took Bethlehem on 6 June. Jerusalem, taken by the Fatimids from the Seljuks only the year before, was besieged from 7 June and stormed on 15 July, when much of its Muslim and Jewish population was massacred. Godfrey of Bouillon was chosen as its ruler, and on 12 August the crusaders routed a Fatimid army from Egypt at Ascalon, although the city itself stayed Fatimid."
  }
